Q: Fastest formula to get Hue from RGB A: <p><em>Fastest</em> is subjective, because it depends on the execution environment (input/output pixel format, CPU, memory, OS, language, runtime platform, algorithm...) which is completely lacking in the question. But as a general rule, it's <em><strong>always</strong></em> faster to do multimedia processing in <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SIMD" rel="nofollow noreferrer">SIMD</a> <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/q/1422149/995714">vectors</a>, because that's the very reason SIMD exists in consumer CPUs. A formula is not important, what's important is its implementation, which decides the speed</p>
<p>Since the question is too broad, below I'll give a very basic C solution using SSE to calculate hue partially in SIMD. The input here is interleaved 8-bit RGB, and output is in float. The conversion from interleaved format is still done in scalar for simplicity, but it's already 9-11 times faster than pure scalar code. The formula to calculate is already provided in other answers, this is just the vectorized part</p>
<p>You can make it <em>a lot</em> faster simply by removing the scalar shuffling part for deinterleaving and do it in SIMD. But you can improve it significantly by various other methods such as</p>
<ul>
<li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Loop_unrolling" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Unroll the loops</a></li>
<li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prefetching" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Prefetch</a></li>
<li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Direct_memory_access" rel="nofollow noreferrer">DMA</a></li>
<li>Change input format, for example RGBA input instead of RGB input, or have planar (SoA) input instead of interleaved pixel channels (AoS)</li>
<li>Use <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bfloat16_floating-point_format" rel="nofollow noreferrer">bfloat16</a> or <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Half-precision_floating-point_format" rel="nofollow noreferrer">half float</a> instead of float</li>
<li><a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parallel_computing" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Parallelize</a></li>
<li>Use newer SIMD ISA (AVX2, AVX-512, SVE...) with longer vector, newer instructions...</li>
<li>Use other HW accelerators like GPU, ASIC or DSP (TI, Hexagon...)</li>
<li>...</li>
</ul>
<p>A speed up of 20-40x is absolutely normal, with more room if you have more cores to spare, because the hue calculation is completely scalable. It'll be far faster than any other answers in this question</p>
<p>Here's the code</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>void hue_scalar_interleaved(const uint8_t* rgb, float* hue, int count)
{
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; count; ++i) {
        hue[i] = hue_scalar_pixel(rgb[3 * i + 0], rgb[3 * i + 1], rgb[3 * i + 2]);
    }
}

// Simple SSE4.1 version: deinterleave using scalar code,
// processes 4 pixels per iteration from interleaved RGB.
void hue_sse41_interleaved(const uint8_t* rgb, float* hue, int count)
{
    const __m128 kZeroF = _mm_set1_ps(0.0f);
    const __m128 kTwo = _mm_set1_ps(2.0f);
    const __m128 kFour = _mm_set1_ps(4.0f);
    const __m128 kSixty = _mm_set1_ps(60.0f);
    const __m128 kThreeSixty = _mm_set1_ps(360.0f);

    int i = 0;
    for (; i + 4 &lt;= count; i += 4) {
        // Deinterleave 4x RGB
        uint8_t r[4], g[4], b[4];
        for (int j = 0; j &lt; 4; ++j) {
            r[j] = rgb[3 * (i + j) + 0];
            g[j] = rgb[3 * (i + j) + 1];
            b[j] = rgb[3 * (i + j) + 2];
        }
        uint32_t rv, gv, bv;
        std::memcpy(&amp;rv, r, 4);
        std::memcpy(&amp;gv, g, 4);
        std::memcpy(&amp;bv, b, 4);

        const __m128i ri32 = _mm_cvtepu8_epi32(_mm_cvtsi32_si128(static_cast&lt;int&gt;(rv)));
        const __m128i gi32 = _mm_cvtepu8_epi32(_mm_cvtsi32_si128(static_cast&lt;int&gt;(gv)));
        const __m128i bi32 = _mm_cvtepu8_epi32(_mm_cvtsi32_si128(static_cast&lt;int&gt;(bv)));

        const __m128 rf = _mm_cvtepi32_ps(ri32);
        const __m128 gf = _mm_cvtepi32_ps(gi32);
        const __m128 bf = _mm_cvtepi32_ps(bi32);

        const __m128 max_rg = _mm_max_ps(rf, gf);
        const __m128 maxc = _mm_max_ps(max_rg, bf);
        const __m128 min_rg = _mm_min_ps(rf, gf);
        const __m128 minc = _mm_min_ps(min_rg, bf);
        const __m128 delta = _mm_sub_ps(maxc, minc);

        const __m128 hr = _mm_div_ps(_mm_sub_ps(gf, bf), delta);
        const __m128 hg = _mm_add_ps(_mm_div_ps(_mm_sub_ps(bf, rf), delta), kTwo);
        const __m128 hb = _mm_add_ps(_mm_div_ps(_mm_sub_ps(rf, gf), delta), kFour);

        const __m128 mask_r = _mm_cmpeq_ps(maxc, rf);
        const __m128 mask_g = _mm_cmpeq_ps(maxc, gf);

        __m128 h = hb;
        h = _mm_blendv_ps(h, hg, mask_g);
        h = _mm_blendv_ps(h, hr, mask_r);
        h = _mm_mul_ps(h, kSixty);

        const __m128 neg_mask = _mm_cmplt_ps(h, kZeroF);
        h = _mm_add_ps(h, _mm_and_ps(neg_mask, kThreeSixty));

        const __m128 zero_delta_mask = _mm_cmpeq_ps(delta, kZeroF);
        h = _mm_blendv_ps(h, kZeroF, zero_delta_mask);

        _mm_storeu_ps(hue + i, h);
    }
    for (; i &lt; count; ++i) {
        hue[i] = hue_scalar_pixel(rgb[3 * i + 0], rgb[3 * i + 1], rgb[3 * i + 2]);
    }
}
</code></pre>
