Q: Refactor validation with return if statement A: <p>I apologize in advance for my unfamiliarity with advanced Java programming, but perhaps my general knowledge can suffice.</p>
<p>I see a couple approaches you could take:</p>
<h3>Exceptions</h3>
<p>Have the validating methods raise exceptions if their error lists are not empty, or write an adapter that does so.</p>
<p>This would, of course, change the interface or your methods, which might not be acceptable (and is, quite understandably, outside the scope of <em>refactoring</em>); but if in the levels &quot;above&quot;, where these methods are called from, you can handle the exceptions gracefully, it's a viable option.</p>
<p>You can have a custom exception class with a field to encapsulate the error list, in order to retrieve it and construct the response based on it.</p>
<h3>Railway-oriented programming</h3>
<p>I genuinely don't think I can do this concept justice in this mere answer. But I've found a few resources that implement the concept in Java, I hope they are of use to you:</p>
<ul>
<li>A <a href="https://github.com/StefanMacke/ao-railway" rel="nofollow noreferrer">GitHub Repository</a></li>
<li>A <a href="https://medium.com/@abhishekshrivastav1920/railway-oriented-programming-rop-in-java-spring-boot-a-practical-guide-for-real-world-projects-7aa2a4dddfbf" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Medium article</a></li>
</ul>
<p>The fundamental idea is to have a &quot;happy&quot; track, where everything goes right, and a single &quot;error&quot; track, that gets diverted into whenever <em>any</em> validation error or unexpected situation occurs.</p>
<p>This concept has a parallel (or maybe it's based on?) monadic programming from functional languages.</p>
<p>Java has had something <em>similar</em> since SE 8, with <code>Optional&lt;T&gt;</code>. You can think of this new approach as an extension of that concept, with a fundamental unit <code>Result&lt;T, E&gt;</code>; <code>Optional&lt;T&gt;</code>, then, becomes a specialization of it, having <code>E = null</code>.</p>
<h3>Middle-term solution</h3>
<p>This would require redistributing responsibilities throughout the classes, but it provides minimal repetition.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>@Service
public class UserService {
    @Autowired
    private UserValidation userValidation;

    // Note the new classes, which would probably be adapters of the old ones.
    // Also, save for the possibility of implementing caching of the error list,
    //  it gets computed twice in this solution (once for checking validity,
    //  and once for producing the failed response).

    // If auto-wiring `userValidation` directly during instantiation of
    //  the `...Request` objects is an option, opt for that.
    //  Otherwise, pass it as a parameter to `.isValid`.

    public BaseResponse&lt;AuthenticationResponse&gt; login(ValidatingUserLoginRequest request) {
        if (!request.isValid()) return request.asFailedResponse();
        // ...
    }

    public BaseResponse&lt;AuthenticationResponse&gt; register(ValidatingUserRegisterRequest request) {
        if (!request.isValid()) return request.asFailedResponse();
        // ...
    }

    public BaseResponse&lt;User&gt; view(ValidatingUserViewRequest username) {
        if (!request.isValid()) return request.asFailedResponse();
        // ...
    }

    public BaseResponse&lt;Void&gt; edit(ValidatingUserEditRequest request) {
        if (!request.isValid()) return request.asFailedResponse();
        // ...
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: How to add timestamp (date) to BenchmarkDotNet exported HTML or CSV file names? A: <p>There is an option for that (<a href="https://benchmarkdotnet.org/articles/configs/configoptions.html?q=DontOverwriteResults" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Config Options</a> doc):</p>
<blockquote>
<p><code>ConfigOptions.DontOverwriteResults</code> - The exported result files should not be overwritten (by default they are overwritten).</p>
</blockquote>
<p>So try adding:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>WithOptions(ConfigOptions.DontOverwriteResults);
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/bZIL934U.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/bZIL934U.png" alt="enter image description here" /></a></p>
<p>Alternatively you can use <code>fileNameSuffix</code>.</p>
<p>Some of standard exporters allows passing <code>fileNameSuffix</code>, for example - <a href="https://benchmarkdotnet.org/articles/configs/exporters.html#sample-introexportxml" rel="nofollow noreferrer">XML</a> and <a href="https://benchmarkdotnet.org/articles/configs/exporters.html#sample-introexportjson" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Json</a> one:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>AddExporter(JsonExporter.Custom(&quot;-custom&quot;, indentJson: true, excludeMeasurements: true));
</code></pre>
<p>For others the suffix property is not exposed but you can inherit it and override the suffix:</p>
<pre><code>public class MyHtmlExporter : HtmlExporter
{
    public MyHtmlExporter()
    {
        FileNameSuffix = $&quot;_gurustron_{DateTime.UtcNow.Ticks}&quot;;
    }

    protected override string FileNameSuffix { get; }
}
</code></pre>
<pre><code>AddExporter(new MyHtmlExporter());
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/4hzw2ytL.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/4hzw2ytL.png" alt="enter image description here" /></a></p>
<p>The same can be done with csv:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>public class MyCsvExporter : CsvExporter
{
    public MyCsvExporter() : base(CsvSeparator.CurrentCulture) // mimic the Default behavior
    {
        FileNameSuffix = $&quot;_gurustron_{DateTime.UtcNow.Ticks}&quot;;
    }

    protected override string FileNameSuffix { get; }
}
</code></pre>
<p>One thing I noticed - not all suffixes actually work, for example <code>DateTime.UtcNow.ToLongTimeString()</code> (instead of <code>DateTime.UtcNow.Ticks</code>) has not worked for me.</p>
